Quick readme on how to build:

  - Get GNU make (gmake on non-GNU systems) and a GNU-compatible C
    toolchain capable of generating x86-64 ELF objects, and add it to
    your path. Your system GCC/binutils or Clang/LLVM/LLD will be more
    than enough if you're using a 64 bit x86 UNIX-like OS.

  - Get nasm, git (used by kernel/get-deps to fetch the kernel's
    dependencies), curl (used to download the Limine release and the
    EDK2 OVMF firmware images) and xorriso. A C compiler for the host
    (cc by default, see the HOST_CC make variable) is also needed to
    build the limine host utility.

  - Get the latest Dawn ZIP from http://users.atw.hu/gerigeri/DawnOS/download.html
    Extract and move the Dawn directory to the root of the repo.

  - Run "make run" in the root of the repository tree. (Needs qemu-system-x86_64 to run).

  - Enjoy.

Toolchain selection:

  The TOOLCHAIN and TOOLCHAIN_PREFIX make variables can be used to set
  the toolchain. TOOLCHAIN can be set to llvm to use Clang/LLVM, for
  example "make TOOLCHAIN=llvm", or "make TOOLCHAIN_PREFIX=x86_64-elf-".

  The kernel is linked through the compiler driver, using its default
  linker for GCC and LLD for Clang. A different linker can be picked by
  adding a -fuse-ld= option to LDFLAGS.

  Link-time optimisation can be enabled by adding -flto to CFLAGS, for
  example "make CFLAGS='-g -O2 -pipe -flto'".

Makefile targets:

  "make all" builds the kernel and a bootable ISO image, and "make all-hdd"
  builds the kernel and a raw image suitable to be flashed onto a USB stick
  or hard drive/SSD, which additionally needs sgdisk (usually from the gdisk
  or gptfdisk packages) and mtools. The size of the raw image, in MiB, is
  set by the HDD_SIZE make variable.

  "make run" and "make run-hdd" build the respective image and run it in
  QEMU. The run-uefi and run-hdd-uefi targets are equivalent, except that
  they boot QEMU using a UEFI-compatible firmware.
